    ($SATQ$b0fb36ad$SATQ$, $SATQ$2995ef09-1643-42cb-8005-8bc49ecc45e7$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Unique among animal species, humans use our vocal apparatuses primarily for two separate communicative purposes: to talk and to sing. The question of what cross-cultural traits distinguish these distinct modes, and secondarily what pressures led humans to develop them in the first place, <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> neuropsychologist Daniela Sammler&rsquo;s 2024 study &ldquo;Signatures of Speech and Song: &lsquo;Universal&rsquo; Links despite Cultural Diversity.&rdquo;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"752408bb-a75f-4396-a1ca-6a7ec480a70d","content":"<p>animates</p>"},{"id":"bfe8ed96-f249-44b3-9e58-4243f9402bb0","content":"<p>have animated</p>"},{"id":"6df979c4-3168-4e91-a588-54d75b69d96b","content":"<p>animate</p>"},{"id":"a5c67e29-d42d-4add-96d6-9cc389dc51fc","content":"<p>animating</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["752408bb-a75f-4396-a1ca-6a7ec480a70d"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-verb agreement. The singular verb "animates" agrees in number with the singular subject "question."</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the plural verb "have animated" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject "question." Choice C is incorrect because the plural verb "animate" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject "question." Choice D is incorrect because it results in an ungrammatical sentence. The nonfinite participle "animating" doesn&rsquo;t supply the clause with a finite verb.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"b0fb36ad","external_id":"2995ef09-1643-42cb-8005-8bc49ecc45e7","vaultid":null,"uId":"c4491354-411f-4b50-98eb-53311f2c3f5f","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Unique among animal species, humans use our vocal apparatuses primarily for two separate communicative purposes: to talk and to sing. The question of what cross-cultural traits distinguish these distinct modes, and secondarily what pressures led humans to develop them in the first place, <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> neuropsychologist Daniela Sammler&rsquo;s 2024 study &ldquo;Signatures of Speech and Song: &lsquo;Universal&rsquo; Links despite Cultural Diversity.&rdquo;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"752408bb-a75f-4396-a1ca-6a7ec480a70d","content":"<p>animates</p>"},{"id":"bfe8ed96-f249-44b3-9e58-4243f9402bb0","content":"<p>have animated</p>"},{"id":"6df979c4-3168-4e91-a588-54d75b69d96b","content":"<p>animate</p>"},{"id":"a5c67e29-d42d-4add-96d6-9cc389dc51fc","content":"<p>animating</p>"}],"keys":["752408bb-a75f-4396-a1ca-6a7ec480a70d"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-verb agreement. The singular verb \"animates\" agrees in number with the singular subject \"question.\"</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the plural verb \"have animated\" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject \"question.\" Choice C is incorrect because the plural verb \"animate\" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject \"question.\" Choice D is incorrect because it results in an ungrammatical sentence. The nonfinite participle \"animating\" doesn&rsquo;t supply the clause with a finite verb.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061176,"updateDate":1755117061176}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061176, 1755117061176),
    ($SATQ$b15724fc$SATQ$, $SATQ$f08c7902-ce6e-41a2-9e7f-ce75f04a265b$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$26885271-6fb1-4396-a146-7005c4fb980a$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>American writer Edwidge Danticat, who emigrated from Haiti in 1981, has won acclaim for her powerful short stories, novels, and <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> her lyrical yet unflinching depictions of her native country&rsquo;s turbulent history, writer Robert Antoni has compared Danticat to Nobel Prize&ndash;winning novelist Toni Morrison. </p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"0e486354-e766-48d1-8692-a2f43cb29652","content":"<p>essays, praising</p>"},{"id":"c87d0078-7b41-429e-9a76-fea8a05d75fd","content":"<p>essays and praising</p>"},{"id":"5f79f4a1-7e43-48bf-82d0-eff44bb2249b","content":"<p>essays praising</p>"},{"id":"4fde161a-6e10-4c74-819b-40adc67537c7","content":"<p>essays. Praising</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["4fde161a-6e10-4c74-819b-40adc67537c7"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation use between sentences. In this choice, the period after &ldquo;essays&rdquo; is used correctly to mark the boundary between one sentence (&ldquo;American&hellip;essays&rdquo;) and another (&ldquo;praising&hellip;Morrison&rdquo;). The participial phrase beginning with &ldquo;Praising&rdquo; modifies the subject of the second sentence, &ldquo;writer Robert Antoni.&rdquo; </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. A comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to mark the boundary between sentences. Choice B is incorrect. Without a comma preceding it, the conjunction &ldquo;and&rdquo; can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join sentences. Choice C is incorrect because it results in a run-on sentence. The sentences (&ldquo;American&hellip;essays&rdquo; and &ldquo;Praising&hellip;Morrison&rdquo;) are fused without punctuation and/or a conjunction. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-600-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$0f9c23d7-2b5c-4d79-b538-0f1c7ab6fc57$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"b15724fc","external_id":"f08c7902-ce6e-41a2-9e7f-ce75f04a265b","vaultid":"26885271-6fb1-4396-a146-7005c4fb980a","uId":"a37bfa11-6fef-4d21-9bb7-436294d10f4d","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>American writer Edwidge Danticat, who emigrated from Haiti in 1981, has won acclaim for her powerful short stories, novels, and <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> her lyrical yet unflinching depictions of her native country&rsquo;s turbulent history, writer Robert Antoni has compared Danticat to Nobel Prize&ndash;winning novelist Toni Morrison. </p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?&nbsp;</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"0e486354-e766-48d1-8692-a2f43cb29652","content":"<p>essays, praising</p>"},{"id":"c87d0078-7b41-429e-9a76-fea8a05d75fd","content":"<p>essays and praising</p>"},{"id":"5f79f4a1-7e43-48bf-82d0-eff44bb2249b","content":"<p>essays praising</p>"},{"id":"4fde161a-6e10-4c74-819b-40adc67537c7","content":"<p>essays. Praising</p>"}],"keys":["4fde161a-6e10-4c74-819b-40adc67537c7"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation use between sentences. In this choice, the period after &ldquo;essays&rdquo; is used correctly to mark the boundary between one sentence (&ldquo;American&hellip;essays&rdquo;) and another (&ldquo;praising&hellip;Morrison&rdquo;). The participial phrase beginning with &ldquo;Praising&rdquo; modifies the subject of the second sentence, &ldquo;writer Robert Antoni.&rdquo; </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. A comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to mark the boundary between sentences. Choice B is incorrect. Without a comma preceding it, the conjunction &ldquo;and&rdquo; can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join sentences. Choice C is incorrect because it results in a run-on sentence. The sentences (&ldquo;American&hellip;essays&rdquo; and &ldquo;Praising&hellip;Morrison&rdquo;) are fused without punctuation and/or a conjunction. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-600-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"0f9c23d7-2b5c-4d79-b538-0f1c7ab6fc57","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959338,"updateDate":1691007959338}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959338, 1691007959338),
    ($SATQ$b1e8b87f$SATQ$, $SATQ$6178f63c-ef9a-4ece-8394-3d77620b7ff1$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$8dd47df5-db17-4e8c-ab76-dd0fac353d7f$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Winding through the ice atop Norway&rsquo;s Jotunheim Mountains is the Lendbreen pass, an ancient route that was used by hunters, farmers, traders, and travelers in the Middle Ages before eventually falling into disuse. Recently, archeologists have <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> knives, horseshoes, wool tunics, and thousands of other artifacts from the icy pass.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"b1a3ad9f-bb9b-4824-a373-bcf41f534b76","content":"<p>unearthed:</p>"},{"id":"3e442e57-d866-4de9-a2d7-62f5259b5a10","content":"<p>unearthed,</p>"},{"id":"c89a185e-8fca-4580-918b-6d2a65c58f9c","content":"<p>unearthed&mdash;</p>"},{"id":"9df978e3-c70c-4a23-89e1-de76525304db","content":"<p>unearthed</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["9df978e3-c70c-4a23-89e1-de76525304db"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. No punctuation should separate the verb "unearthed" and its objects (i.e, what was "unearthed"): "knives, horseshoes, wool tunics, and thousands of other artifacts."</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice creates a punctuation error, separating the verb "unearthed" from its objects (i.e, what was "unearthed") with a colon. Choice B is incorrect. This choice creates a punctuation error, separating the verb "unearthed" from its objects (i.e, what was "unearthed") with a comma. Choice C is incorrect. This choice creates a punctuation error, separating the verb "unearthed" from its objects (i.e, what was "unearthed") with a dash.</p>$SATQ$, false,