objects (i.e, what was \"unearthed\") with a comma. Choice C is incorrect. This choice creates a punctuation error, separating the verb \"unearthed\" from its objects (i.e, what was \"unearthed\") with a dash.</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-BOU-E008","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"b8ace070-6153-4dfd-a99c-96d084d1804e","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959358,"updateDate":1691007959358}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959358, 1691007959358),
    ($SATQ$b260c65a$SATQ$, $SATQ$a36b0b7c-48db-4ea0-9355-d1af147bdc3c$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$59d11af7-cc91-4df8-abdf-15624bebad7f$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Earth is not a perfect sphere. Due to the <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> gravitational pull, Earth bulges out on the sides closest to and farthest from the Moon. This distorting pull is known as a tidal force, and it is responsible for the changes in water levels that are called high and low tides.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"57b3631d-2032-4a38-8654-84afe7a2e537","content":"<p>Moon&rsquo;s</p>"},{"id":"a0f9edeb-b351-42af-957f-49f526698e84","content":"<p>Moons</p>"},{"id":"d7306a4a-89a3-41e5-bd52-f82d3eed1e3c","content":"<p>Moons&rsquo;</p>"},{"id":"ed6834ae-8c28-45c9-a10b-984f8dbe06d5","content":"<p>Moon</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["57b3631d-2032-4a38-8654-84afe7a2e537"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of possessive nouns. The singular possessive noun "Moon&rsquo;s" correctly indicates that there is only one Moon, and it has a gravitational pull.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the context requires the singular possessive noun "Moon&rsquo;s," not the plural noun "Moons." Choice C is incorrect because the context requires the singular possessive noun "Moon&rsquo;s," not the plural possessive noun "Moons&rsquo;." Choice D is incorrect because the context requires the singular possessive noun "Moon&rsquo;s," not the singular noun "Moon."</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-FSS-E009$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$e4e072a6-b6fe-4971-baf3-e953c2d4398c$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$S