    ($SATQ$b35cefb7$SATQ$, $SATQ$0dbd2984-0698-425d-b3f3-72eec63753e7$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$1dd884fd-e085-4b54-948e-f7563c5f6e93$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The fine, powdery substance that covers the Moon&rsquo;s surface is called regolith. Because regolith is both readily available and high in oxygen <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> scientists have wondered whether it could be used as a potential source of oxygen for future lunar settlements.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"4a71b22c-abe4-4a43-ac3c-be1428f8bf9c","content":"<p>content and</p>"},{"id":"e0981b74-b4b8-468d-bdd3-60abe828326f","content":"<p>content,</p>"},{"id":"901117d6-45bb-469a-a36c-a46208a1a285","content":"<p>content</p>"},{"id":"e9c4c7fe-98a1-4811-ab38-a265847dcbba","content":"<p>content, and</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["e0981b74-b4b8-468d-bdd3-60abe828326f"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation between a subordinate clause and a main clause. This choice correctly uses a comma to mark the boundary between the subordinate clause (&ldquo;Because...content&rdquo;) and the main clause (&ldquo;scientists...settlements&rdquo;). </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Joining the subordinate clause (&ldquo;Because...content&rdquo;) and the clause that follows (&ldquo;scientists...settlements&rdquo;) with the conjunction &ldquo;and&rdquo; results in an ungrammatical sentence that lacks a main clause. Choice C is incorrect because it fails to mark the boundary between the subordinate clause and the main clause with appropriate punctuation. Choice D is incorrect. Joining the subordinate clause (&ldquo;Because...content&rdquo;) and the clause that follows (&ldquo;scientists...settlements&rdquo;) with a comma and the conjunction &ldquo;and&rdquo; results in an ungrammatical sentence that lacks a main clause. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-713-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$4fbb188d-6200-4881-b17a-6fb782a48d7b$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"b35cefb7","external_id":"0dbd2984-0698-425d-b3f3-72eec63753e7","vaultid":"1dd884fd-e085-4b54-948e-f7563c5f6e93","uId":"40f12b3a-8681-418d-905f-20ffaec58a24","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The fine, powdery substance that covers the Moon&rsquo;s surface is called regolith. Because regolith is both readily available and high in oxygen <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> scientists have wondered whether it could be used as a potential source of oxygen for future lunar settlements.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"4a71b22c-abe4-4a43-ac3c-be1428f8bf9c","content":"<p>content and</p>"},{"id":"e0981b74-b4b8-468d-bdd3-60abe828326f","content":"<p>content,</p>"},{"id":"901117d6-45bb-469a-a36c-a46208a1a285","content":"<p>content</p>"},{"id":"e9c4c7fe-98a1-4811-ab38-a265847dcbba","content":"<p>content, and</p>"}],"keys":["e0981b74-b4b8-468d-bdd3-60abe828326f"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation between a subordinate clause and a main clause. This choice correctly uses a comma to mark the boundary between the subordinate clause (&ldquo;Because...content&rdquo;) and the main clause (&ldquo;scientists...settlements&rdquo;). </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Joining the subordinate clause (&ldquo;Because...content&rdquo;) and the clause that follows (&ldquo;scientists...settlements&rdquo;) with the conjunction &ldquo;and&rdquo; results in an ungrammatical sentence that lacks a main clause. Choice C is incorrect because it fails to mark the boundary between the subordinate clause and the main clause with appropriate punctuation. Choice D is incorrect. Joining the subordinate clause (&ldquo;Because...content&rdquo;) and the clause that follows (&ldquo;scientists...settlements&rdquo;) with a comma and the conjunction &ldquo;and&rdquo; results in an ungrammatical sentence that lacks a main clause. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-713-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"4fbb188d-6200-4881-b17a-6fb782a48d7b","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959348,"updateDate":1691007959348}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959348, 1691007959348),
    ($SATQ$b369d54c$SATQ$, $SATQ$9ab447b4-2056-48f4-8c73-e99de88bddef$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Woven from recycled yarn and hand tufted using a carpet weaving technique passed down by the artist&rsquo;s Turkish grandmother, <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> so lush and tactilely inviting that you are tempted to reach out and touch them.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"71296cf5-42d7-48d7-a645-fea960aa1ac5","content":"<p>the topological tapestries of Argentine textile artist Alexandra Kehayoglou are</p>"},{"id":"6ec63424-b338-41a7-8304-0e644f23dc8e","content":"<p>the Argentine textile artist Alexandra Kehayoglou creates topological tapestries that are</p>"},{"id":"41898ea5-5e47-41f9-a50f-5d82250c2227","content":"<p>when she creates her topological tapestries, Argentine textile artist Alexandra Kehayoglou makes them</p>"},{"id":"571954d6-7d82-48eb-bf15-eec22c2e1208","content":"<p>Alexandra Kehayoglou is an Argentine textile artist whose topological tapestries are</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["71296cf5-42d7-48d7-a645-fea960aa1ac5"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-modifier placement. This choice makes the noun phrase "topological tapestries" the subject of the sentence and places it immediately after the modifying phrase "woven&hellip;grandmother." In doing so, this choice clearly establishes that the topological tapestries&mdash;and not another noun in the sentence&mdash;are being described as woven from recycled yarn and hand tufted.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement 