    ($SATQ$b5ff8f8e$SATQ$, $SATQ$5e58fdff-e882-4add-a135-ba9fbcd54abf$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The element carbon has the highest melting point <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> all the elements on the periodic table&mdash;3,500 degrees Celsius.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"12121d26-f43c-4bd9-958a-f671fef14076","content":"<p>of</p>"},{"id":"4278f4d0-dfff-4bd9-be5b-327c24639924","content":"<p>of&mdash;</p>"},{"id":"a491b2cb-fe52-4600-8923-e23df0bcc827","content":"<p>of,</p>"},{"id":"0f738e57-a2e7-45d4-95c2-2ca721c5beb4","content":"<p>of:</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["12121d26-f43c-4bd9-958a-f671fef14076"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation use between a preposition and its complement. No punctuation is needed between the preposition "of" and its complement "all the elements on the periodic table." The complement completes the meaning of the preposition in the phrase "the highest melting point of all the elements on the periodic table," and using punctuation to separate the complement from the preposition results in an ungrammatical sentence.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the preposition and its complement. Choice C is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the preposition and its complement. Choice D is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the preposition and its complement.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"b5ff8f8e","external_id":"5e58fdff-e882-4add-a135-ba9fbcd54abf","vaultid":null,"uId":"d7446e72-47c6-46be-aaec-858fa6615513","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":1,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The element carbon has the highest melting point <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> all the elements on the periodic table&mdash;3,500 degrees Celsius.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"12121d26-f43c-4bd9-958a-f671fef14076","content":"<p>of</p>"},{"id":"4278f4d0-dfff-4bd9-be5b-327c24639924","content":"<p>of&mdash;</p>"},{"id":"a491b2cb-fe52-4600-8923-e23df0bcc827","content":"<p>of,</p>"},{"id":"0f738e57-a2e7-45d4-95c2-2ca721c5beb4","content":"<p>of:</p>"}],"keys":["12121d26-f43c-4bd9-958a-f671fef14076"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation use between a preposition and its complement. No punctuation is needed between the preposition \"of\" and its complement \"all the elements on the periodic table.\" The complement completes the meaning of the preposition in the phrase \"the highest melting point of all the elements on the periodic table,\" and using punctuation to separate the complement from the preposition results in an ungrammatical sentence.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the preposition and its complement. Choice C is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the preposition and its complement. Choice D is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the preposition and its complement.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323047,"updateDate":1730147323047}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323047, 1730147323047),
    ($SATQ$b6560e5a$SATQ$, $SATQ$27832d9c-52c0-451d-9ea9-b7f14498c1fd$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$f54eb2a2-467e-466e-a693-07e4cdf90440$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Materials scientist Marie-Agathe Charpagne and her colleagues believed they could improve on the multicomponent alloy NiCoCr, an equal-proportions mixture of nickel (Ni), cobalt (Co), and chromium (Cr), by replacing chromium with ruthenium <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> the alloy that resulted, NiCoRu, turned out to be an unsuitable replacement for NiCoCr.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"6d4ecf23-4d50-495f-ae0a-a68397bccec4","content":"<p>(Ru)</p>"},{"id":"09613def-394d-479f-9374-76b48aab819c","content":"<p>(Ru) but</p>"},{"id":"fcb05c08-e9e9-4c40-bbf1-c7e8dec2f339","content":"<p>(Ru),</p>"},{"id":"07b2cf1d-10ed-4e15-b684-a1a21bfab88a","content":"<p>(Ru), but</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["07b2cf1d-10ed-4e15-b684-a1a21bfab88a"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is the coordination of main clauses. This choice correctly uses a comma and the coordinating conjunction &ldquo;but&rdquo; to join the first main clause (&ldquo;Materials&hellip;Ru&rdquo;) and the second main clause (&ldquo;the alloy&hellip;NiCoCr&rdquo;). </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a run-on sentence. The two main clauses are fused without punctuation and/or a conjunction.&nbsp;Choice B is incorrect because when coordinating two longer main clauses such as these, it&rsquo;s conventional to use a comma before the coordinating conjunction.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. Without a conjunction following it, a comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join two main clauses.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-679-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$3e882a43-aa3e-445d-b8d9-3370e2f44f01$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"b6560e5a","external_id":"27832d9c-52c0-451d-9ea9-b7f14498c1fd","vaultid":"f54eb2a2-467e-466e-a693-07e4cdf90440","uId":"7f8e2946-350f-4207-b45b-afaa94d72d4e","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Materials scientist Marie-Agathe Charpagne and her colleagues believed they could improve on the multicomponent alloy NiCoCr, an equal-proportions mixture of nickel (Ni), cobalt (Co), and chromium (Cr), by replacing chromium with ruthenium <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> the alloy that resulted, NiCoRu, turned out to be an unsuitable replacement for NiCoCr.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"6d4ecf23-4d50-495f-ae0a-a68397bccec4","content":"<p>(Ru)</p>"},{"id":"09613def-394d-479f-9374-76b48aab819c","content":"<p>(Ru) but</p>"},{"id":"fcb05c08-e9e9-4c40-bbf1-c7e8dec2f339","content":"<p>(Ru),</p>"},{"id":"07b2cf1d-10ed-4e15-b684-a1a21bfab88a","content":"<p>(Ru), but</p>"}],"keys":["07b2cf1d-10ed-4e15-b684-a1a21bfab88a"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is the coordination of main clauses. This choice correctly uses a comma and the coordinating conjunction &ldquo;but&rdquo; to join the first main clause (&ldquo;Materials&hellip;Ru&rdquo;) and the second main clause (&ldquo;the alloy&hellip;NiCoCr&rdquo;). </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a run-on sentence. The two main clauses are fused without punctuation and/or a conjunction.&nbsp;Choice B is incorrect because when coordinating two longer main clauses such as these, it&rsquo;s conventional to use a comma before the coordinating conjunction.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. Without a conjunction following it, a comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join two main clauses.&nbsp;</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-679-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"3e882a43-aa3e-445d-b8d9-3370e2f44f01","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959346,"updateDate":1691007959346}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959346, 1691007959346),
    ($SATQ$b6de636f$SATQ$, $SATQ$023cedea-3308-47d9-a9ec-781faa676e63$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>At 1,377 meters, the Tsing Ma Bridge in  China is one of the longest suspension bridges in the <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> the Golden Gate Bridge in the United States, at 1,280 meters, is even longer.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"2fd377db-245b-4535-b6b3-e00312ca8fb3","content":"<p>world but</p>"},{"id":"aafd83c4-3423-40ff-a361-f65dbf45eb81","content":"<p>world<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span> but<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span></p>"},{"id":"c5a1f98b-2fd2-4e1c-9526-62a262cdc53e","content":"<p>world<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span></p>"},{"id":"65835cac-6c1a-4fa0-963b-58d0332aedff","content":"<p>world<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span> but</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["65835cac-6c1a-4fa0-963b-58d0332aedff"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is the coordination of main clauses within a sentence. This choice correctly uses a comma and the coordinating conjunction "but" to join the first main clause ("At...world") and the second main clause ("the...longer").</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because when coordinating two main clauses such as these, it&rsquo;s conventional to use a comma before the coordinating conjunction. Choice B is incorrect because a comma isn&rsquo;t needed after the conjunction "but." Choice C is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. Without a conjunction following it, a comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join two main clauses.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"b6de636f","external_id":"023cedea-3308-47d9-a9ec-781faa676e63","vaultid":null,"uId":"c2412359-1772-4a48-a7a3-8010c3e1c72d","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>At 1,377 meters, the Tsing Ma Bridge in  China is one of the longest suspension bridges in the <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> the Golden Gate Bridge in the United States, at 1,280 meters, is even longer.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"2fd377db-245b-4535-b6b3-e00312ca8fb3","content":"<p>world but</p>"},{"id":"aafd83c4-3423-40ff-a361-f65dbf45eb81","content":"<p>world<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span> but<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span></p>"},{"id":"c5a1f98b-2fd2-4e1c-9526-62a262cdc53e","content":"<p>world<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span></p>"},{"id":"65835cac-6c1a-4fa0-963b-58d0332aedff","content":"<p>world<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span> but</p>"}],"keys":["65835cac-6c1a-4fa0-963b-58d0332aedff"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is the coordination of main clauses within a sentence. This choice correctly uses a comma and the coordinating conjunction \"but\" to join the first main clause (\"At...world\") and the second main clause (\"the...longer\").</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because when coordinating two main clauses such as these, it&rsquo;s conventional to use a comma before the coordinating conjunction. Choice B is incorrect because a comma isn&rsquo;t needed after the conjunction \"but.\" Choice C is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. Without a conjunction following it, a comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join two main clauses.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061175,"updateDate":1755117061175}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061175, 1755117061175),
    ($SATQ$b7363ba2$SATQ$, $SATQ$4013bf79-48e1-43dc-b34f-7b74a15c2005$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$f66dc93c-5973-4205-b2c9-a9a1838c608f$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Mathematician and meteorologist Edward Lorenz used the metaphor of the &ldquo;butterfly effect&rdquo; to explain how seemingly minor events can have major impacts on future weather. According to Lorenz&rsquo;s metaphor, the wind from a butterfly flapping <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> in Brazil might eventually grow into a storm elsewhere across the globe.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a6e8eb8c-05be-402e-bc32-6389116ceafd","content":"<p>its wings</p>"},{"id":"9a6dc76f-2f7a-4544-aff0-a695b6f8d2ed","content":"<p>its wings&rsquo;</p>"},{"id":"f876f835-7c17-46df-9086-7dbd7d4210a9","content":"<p>it&rsquo;s wing&rsquo;s</p>"},{"id":"4439b4eb-18dd-48d6-963f-dd9257dddfa5","content":"<p>it&rsquo;s wings&rsquo;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["a6e8eb8c-05be-402e-bc32-6389116ceafd"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The conventions being tested are the use of possessive determiners and plural nouns. The singular possessive determiner "its" and the plural noun "wings" correctly indicate that the butterfly has multiple wings.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the context requires the plural noun "wings," not the plural possessive noun "wings&rsquo;." Choice C is incorrect because the context requires the singular possessive determiner "its" and the plural noun "wings," not the contraction "it&rsquo;s" and the singular possessive noun "wing&rsquo;s." Choice D is incorrect because the context requires the singular possessive determiner "its" and the plural noun "wings," not the contraction "it&rsquo;s" and the plural possessive noun "wings&rsquo;."</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-760-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$2bd368f9-a19b-42b6-a7bd-2643ba2f5cf6$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"b7363ba2","external_id":"4013bf79-48e1-43dc-b34f-7b74a15c2005","vaultid":"f66dc93c-5973-4205-b2c9-a9a1838c608f","uId":"0ad0d784-177d-481c-8e4c-070b93270b2e","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Mathematician and meteorologist Edward Lorenz used the metaphor of the &ldquo;butterfly effect&rdquo; to explain how seemingly minor events can have major impacts on future weather. According to Lorenz&rsquo;s metaphor, the wind from a butterfly flapping <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> in Brazil might eventually grow into a storm elsewhere across the globe.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"a6e8eb8c-05be-402e-bc32-6389116ceafd","content":"<p>its wings</p>"},{"id":"9a6dc76f-2f7a-4544-aff0-a695b6f8d2ed","content":"<p>its wings&rsquo;</p>"},{"id":"f876f835-7c17-46df-9086-7dbd7d4210a9","content":"<p>it&rsquo;s wing&rsquo;s</p>"},{"id":"4439b4eb-18dd-48d6-963f-dd9257dddfa5","content":"<p>it&rsquo;s wings&rsquo;</p>"}],"keys":["a6e8eb8c-05be-402e-bc32-6389116ceafd"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The conventions being tested are the use of possessive determiners and plural nouns. The singular possessive determiner \"its\" and the plural noun \"wings\" correctly indicate that the butterfly has multiple wings.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the context requires the plural noun \"wings,\" not the plural possessive noun \"wings&rsquo;.\" Choice C is incorrect because the context requires the singular possessive determiner \"its\" and the plural noun \"wings,\" not the contraction \"it&rsquo;s\" and the singular possessive noun \"wing&rsquo;s.\" Choice D is incorrect because the context requires the singular possessive determiner \"its\" and the plural noun \"wings,\" not the contraction \"it&rsquo;s\" and the plural possessive noun \"wings&rsquo;.\"</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-760-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"2bd368f9-a19b-42b6-a7bd-2643ba2f5cf6","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959351,"updateDate":1691007959351}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959351, 1691007959351),
    ($SATQ$b74f676f$SATQ$, $SATQ$97896a38-0f18-411b-9cdf-1f1767d325f2$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$493227df-5c6d-4ea3-a8ae-94d7bd9ce834$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Classical composer Florence Price&rsquo;s 1927 move to Chicago marked a turning point in her career. It was there that Price premiered her First Symphony&mdash;a piece that was praised for blending traditional Romantic motifs with aspects of Black folk music&mdash;and <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> supportive relationships with other Black artists.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"2aad4bc4-3dd2-4af1-8fae-4fd1d0184b1b","content":"<p>developing</p>"},{"id":"95271b4e-353b-40ae-aeb0-1580b078b8f6","content":"<p>developed</p>"},{"id":"9100707c-ce2f-4443-95f2-163a1d948387","content":"<p>to develop</p>"},{"id":"26d2689b-7fcb-472a-941c-d7fb761fa910","content":"<p>having developed</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["95271b4e-353b-40ae-aeb0-1580b078b8f6"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The missing verb is part of the same clause as the verb "premiered," and "Price" is the subject of both. So we need the past-tense form "developed" in order to match "premiered."</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice creates a verb form error. The missing verb is part of the same clause as the verb "premiered," and "Price" is the subject of both. So we need the past-tense form "developed" in order to match "premiered." Choice C is incorrect. This choice creates a verb form error. The missing verb is part of the same clause as the verb "premiered," and "Price" is the subject of both. So we need the past-tense form "developed" in order to match "premiered." Choice D is incorrect. This choice creates a verb form error. The missing verb is part of the same clause as the verb "premiered," and "Price" is the subject of both. So we need the past-tense form "developed" in order to match "premiered."</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-FSS-M003$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$341820e4-d34d-4334-b295-92520d0dd1c6$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"b74f676f","external_id":"97896a38-0f18-411b-9cdf-1f1767d325f2","vaultid":"493227df-5c6d-4ea3-a8ae-94d7bd9ce834","uId":"422e5078-9905-43c8-b38b-94104fb1a9c4","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Classical composer Florence Price&rsquo;s 1927 move to Chicago marked a turning point in her career. It was there that Price premiered her First Symphony&mdash;a piece that was praised for blending traditional Romantic motifs with aspects of Black folk music&mdash;and <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> supportive relationships with other Black artists.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"2aad4bc4-3dd2-4af1-8fae-4fd1d0184b1b","content":"<p>developing</p>"},{"id":"95271b4e-353b-40ae-aeb0-1580b078b8f6","content":"<p>developed</p>"},{"id":"9100707c-ce2f-4443-95f2-163a1d948387","content":"<p>to develop</p>"},{"id":"26d2689b-7fcb-472a-941c-d7fb761fa910","content":"<p>having developed</p>"}],"keys":["95271b4e-353b-40ae-aeb0-1580b078b8f6"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. The missing verb is part of the same clause as the verb \"premiered,\" and \"Price\" is the subject of both. So we need the past-tense form \"developed\" in order to match \"premiered.\"</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice creates a verb form error. The missing verb is part of the same clause as the verb \"premiered,\" and \"Price\" is the subject of both. So we need the past-tense form \"developed\" in order to match \"premiered.\" Choice C is incorrect. This choice creates a verb form error. The missing verb is part of the same clause as the verb \"premiered,\" and \"Price\" is the subject of both. So we need the past-tense form \"developed\" in order to match \"premiered.\" Choice D is incorrect. This choice creates a verb form error. The missing verb is part of the same clause as the verb \"premiered,\" and \"Price\" is the subject of both. So we need the past-tense form \"developed\" in order to match \"premiered.\"</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-FSS-M003","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"341820e4-d34d-4334-b295-92520d0dd1c6","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959367,"updateDate":1691007959367}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959367, 1691007959367),
    ($SATQ$b85c19ed$SATQ$, $SATQ$5bd954f5-6874-4280-909d-d887739ee539$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$ec98ed1a-248a-46d9-bed7-13a61d032fe6$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The violins handmade in the seventeenth century by Italian craftsman Antonio Stradivari have been celebrated as some of the finest in the world. In close collaboration with musicians, Stradivari introduced changes to the shape of a traditional violin, flattening some of the instrument&rsquo;s curves and making <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> lighter overall.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"d4fe9e25-425d-4ef6-b989-36afed34b558","content":"<p>those</p>"},{"id":"35685f61-565b-46ee-9b6a-a01ca4f101e6","content":"<p>one</p>"},{"id":"7bd82ab9-5326-4e12-8554-a42e11431e11","content":"<p>them</p>"},{"id":"c9151a5e-6ccb-4671-be91-264f896dd823","content":"<p>it</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["c9151a5e-6ccb-4671-be91-264f896dd823"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is pronoun&ndash;antecedent agreement. The singular pronoun &ldquo;it&rdquo; agrees in number with the singular antecedent &ldquo;violin&rdquo; and thus indicates that the traditional violin (and not its curves) was made lighter. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the plural pronoun &ldquo;those&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular antecedent &ldquo;violin.&rdquo; Choice B is incorrect because the singular pronoun &ldquo;one&rdquo; is ambiguous in this context; the resulting sentence leaves unclear what Stradivari made lighter.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect because the plural pronoun &ldquo;them&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular antecedent &ldquo;violin.&rdquo; </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-638-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$5aaa266a-b62d-4105-8e27-f5a346bedf00$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"b85c19ed","external_id":"5bd954f5-6874-4280-909d-d887739ee539","vaultid":"ec98ed1a-248a-46d9-bed7-13a61d032fe6","uId":"d7281b2e-b5ad-4914-ab0b-65e47edb5e13","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The violins handmade in the seventeenth century by Italian craftsman Antonio Stradivari have been celebrated as some of the finest in the world. In close collaboration with musicians, Stradivari introduced changes to the shape of a traditional violin, flattening some of the instrument&rsquo;s curves and making <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> lighter overall.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"d4fe9e25-425d-4ef6-b989-36afed34b558","content":"<p>those</p>"},{"id":"35685f61-565b-46ee-9b6a-a01ca4f101e6","content":"<p>one</p>"},{"id":"7bd82ab9-5326-4e12-8554-a42e11431e11","content":"<p>them</p>"},{"id":"c9151a5e-6ccb-4671-be91-264f896dd823","content":"<p>it</p>"}],"keys":["c9151a5e-6ccb-4671-be91-264f896dd823"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is pronoun&ndash;antecedent agreement. The singular pronoun &ldquo;it&rdquo; agrees in number with the singular antecedent &ldquo;violin&rdquo; and thus indicates that the traditional violin (and not its curves) was made lighter. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the plural pronoun &ldquo;those&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular antecedent &ldquo;violin.&rdquo; Choice B is incorrect because the singular pronoun &ldquo;one&rdquo; is ambiguous in this context; the resulting sentence leaves unclear what Stradivari made lighter.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect because the plural pronoun &ldquo;them&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular antecedent &ldquo;violin.&rdquo; </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-638-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"5aaa266a-b62d-4105-8e27-f5a346bedf00","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959340,"updateDate":1691007959340}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959340, 1691007959340),
    ($SATQ$b8e13a74$SATQ$, $SATQ$f3591c75-f613-4bdc-a206-484513e1f5e0$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Josephine Paddock and Florence Est&eacute; were among the 300 artists who exhibited at the <span data-ssml-say-as="date">1913</span> Armory Show, a groundbreaking New York City art exhibition that introduced modernism to American audiences. Though shocking at the time, an abstract cubist painting exhibited by Marcel Duchamp<em>&mdash;</em>one of several works that received scorn from critics&mdash;<span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> the Western art canon more than a century later.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"417dd400-22c8-4571-a6ac-ccd1dc440745","content":"<p>has entered&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"253268a6-dec0-4359-ac98-2a4bfa804227","content":"<p>have entered</p>"},{"id":"11fdf6b8-5fab-4340-8996-ea5c983f190f","content":"<p>were entering</p>"},{"id":"93bed14e-2859-42d1-8e8b-152112f3fb9d","content":"<p>enter</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["417dd400-22c8-4571-a6ac-ccd1dc440745"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-verb agreement. The singular verb "has entered" agrees in number with the singular subject "an abstract cubist painting."</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the plural verb "have entered" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject "an abstract cubist painting." Choice C is incorrect because the plural verb "were entering" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject "an abstract cubist painting." Choice D is incorrect because the plural verb "enter" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject "an abstract cubist painting."</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"b8e13a74","external_id":"f3591c75-f613-4bdc-a206-484513e1f5e0","vaultid":null,"uId":"f6af1595-74b3-4f92-abe7-c825a669d489","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Josephine Paddock and Florence Est&eacute; were among the 300 artists who exhibited at the <span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">1913</span> Armory Show, a groundbreaking New York City art exhibition that introduced modernism to American audiences. Though shocking at the time, an abstract cubist painting exhibited by Marcel Duchamp<em>&mdash;</em>one of several works that received scorn from critics&mdash;<span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> the Western art canon more than a century later.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"417dd400-22c8-4571-a6ac-ccd1dc440745","content":"<p>has entered&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"253268a6-dec0-4359-ac98-2a4bfa804227","content":"<p>have entered</p>"},{"id":"11fdf6b8-5fab-4340-8996-ea5c983f190f","content":"<p>were entering</p>"},{"id":"93bed14e-2859-42d1-8e8b-152112f3fb9d","content":"<p>enter</p>"}],"keys":["417dd400-22c8-4571-a6ac-ccd1dc440745"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-verb agreement. The singular verb \"has entered\" agrees in number with the singular subject \"an abstract cubist painting.\"</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the plural verb \"have entered\" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject \"an abstract cubist painting.\" Choice C is incorrect because the plural verb \"were entering\" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject \"an abstract cubist painting.\" Choice D is incorrect because the plural verb \"enter\" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject \"an abstract cubist painting.\"</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061192,"updateDate":1755117061192}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061192, 1755117061192),
    ($SATQ$ba8ebf49$SATQ$, $SATQ$a171e670-9a31-475a-924f-3700761b490b$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$fa787dd8-3411-4f82-b783-7df12ce9fede$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The poem <em>Beowulf</em> begins with the word &ldquo;<span lang="ang">hw&aelig;t</span>,&rdquo; which is an Old English <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> as &ldquo;hark!&rdquo; or &ldquo;listen!&rdquo; in some versions, the word was playfully rendered as &ldquo;bro!&rdquo; by Maria Dahvana Headley in her 2020 translation of the poem.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"b2e65232-8811-4ddd-a117-8a35d80a3cad","content":"<p>exclamation, translated</p>"},{"id":"8305eadc-383f-4da8-a276-b1b98ae8a0e9","content":"<p>exclamation and translated</p>"},{"id":"565ad7d2-eaa7-4545-8890-93b359e2fe24","content":"<p>exclamation translated</p>"},{"id":"575810b3-1070-4346-a0c0-0cbb328d40ba","content":"<p>exclamation. Translated</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["575810b3-1070-4346-a0c0-0cbb328d40ba"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation use between sentences. In this choice, the period is used correctly to mark the boundary between one sentence ("The poem&hellip;exclamation") and another sentence that begins with a supplementary element ("Translated&hellip;poem"). The supplementary element "translated as &lsquo;hark!&rsquo; or &lsquo;listen!&rsquo; in some versions" modifies the subject of the second sentence, "the word" (referring to <span lang="oe">hw&aelig;t</span>).</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a comma splice after "exclamation." A comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to mark the boundary between sentences. Choice B is incorrect. Without a comma preceding it, the conjunction "and" can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join sentences. Choice C is incorrect because it results in a comma splice after "versions." A comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to mark the boundary between sentences.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-752-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$4909a63b-f83c-4465-acad-21ed50383ae8$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"ba8ebf49","external_id":"a171e670-9a31-475a-924f-3700761b490b","vaultid":"fa787dd8-3411-4f82-b783-7df12ce9fede","uId":"8e37ccf1-f87c-4396-b4de-2b7d742dc20f","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The poem <em>Beowulf</em> begins with the word &ldquo;<span lang=\"ang\">hw&aelig;t</span>,&rdquo; which is an Old English <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> as &ldquo;hark!&rdquo; or &ldquo;listen!&rdquo; in some versions, the word was playfully rendered as &ldquo;bro!&rdquo; by Maria Dahvana Headley in her 2020 translation of the poem.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"b2e65232-8811-4ddd-a117-8a35d80a3cad","content":"<p>exclamation, translated</p>"},{"id":"8305eadc-383f-4da8-a276-b1b98ae8a0e9","content":"<p>exclamation and translated</p>"},{"id":"565ad7d2-eaa7-4545-8890-93b359e2fe24","content":"<p>exclamation translated</p>"},{"id":"575810b3-1070-4346-a0c0-0cbb328d40ba","content":"<p>exclamation. Translated</p>"}],"keys":["575810b3-1070-4346-a0c0-0cbb328d40ba"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation use between sentences. In this choice, the period is used correctly to mark the boundary between one sentence (\"The poem&hellip;exclamation\") and another sentence that begins with a supplementary element (\"Translated&hellip;poem\"). The supplementary element \"translated as &lsquo;hark!&rsquo; or &lsquo;listen!&rsquo; in some versions\" modifies the subject of the second sentence, \"the word\" (referring to <span lang=\"oe\">hw&aelig;t</span>).</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a comma splice after \"exclamation.\" A comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to mark the boundary between sentences. Choice B is incorrect. Without a comma preceding it, the conjunction \"and\" can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join sentences. Choice C is incorrect because it results in a comma splice after \"versions.\" A comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to mark the boundary between sentences.</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-752-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"4909a63b-f83c-4465-acad-21ed50383ae8","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959350,"updateDate":1691007959350}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959350, 1691007959350),
    ($SATQ$bb4557cf$SATQ$, $SATQ$f155ec07-5374-4ced-81d2-5bb890c07633$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The relationship between genomes and epigenomes reveals how cells with identical DNA develop different <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> whereas the genome in each cell contains a complete DNA sequence, the epigenome consists of chemical compounds that determine which traits in the sequence will be expressed.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"eccb9301-645d-486c-b17d-efe3bff56d81","content":"<p>functions</p>"},{"id":"48f58b39-5388-45bf-8609-939e45fb976d","content":"<p>functions,</p>"},{"id":"70abaefc-2957-4e68-8753-e3390f98b69d","content":"<p>functions and,</p>"},{"id":"3b701497-32f3-43cf-891a-56645679089e","content":"<p>functions:</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["3b701497-32f3-43cf-891a-56645679089e"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of a colon within a sentence. In this choice, the colon correctly introduces the following explanation of the different functions of genomes and epigenomes.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a run-on sentence. The two main clauses ("The relationship&hellip;functions" and "whereas&hellip;expressed") are fused without punctuation and/or a conjunction. Choice B is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. A comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join two main clauses ("The relationship&hellip;functions" and "whereas&hellip;expressed"). Choice C is incorrect. Without a comma preceding it, the conjunction "and" can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join two main clauses. Furthermore, "and" fails to indicate that what follows is an explanation of how cells with identical DNA develop different functions.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"bb4557cf","external_id":"f155ec07-5374-4ced-81d2-5bb890c07633","vaultid":null,"uId":"e139e397-5be6-4ad0-9422-f661165d6369","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The relationship between genomes and epigenomes reveals how cells with identical DNA develop different <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> whereas the genome in each cell contains a complete DNA sequence, the epigenome consists of chemical compounds that determine which traits in the sequence will be expressed.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"eccb9301-645d-486c-b17d-efe3bff56d81","content":"<p>functions</p>"},{"id":"48f58b39-5388-45bf-8609-939e45fb976d","content":"<p>functions,</p>"},{"id":"70abaefc-2957-4e68-8753-e3390f98b69d","content":"<p>functions and,</p>"},{"id":"3b701497-32f3-43cf-891a-56645679089e","content":"<p>functions:</p>"}],"keys":["3b701497-32f3-43cf-891a-56645679089e"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of a colon within a sentence. In this choice, the colon correctly introduces the following explanation of the different functions of genomes and epigenomes.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a run-on sentence. The two main clauses (\"The relationship&hellip;functions\" and \"whereas&hellip;expressed\") are fused without punctuation and/or a conjunction. Choice B is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. A comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join two main clauses (\"The relationship&hellip;functions\" and \"whereas&hellip;expressed\"). Choice C is incorrect. Without a comma preceding it, the conjunction \"and\" can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join two main clauses. Furthermore, \"and\" fails to indicate that what follows is an explanation of how cells with identical DNA develop different functions.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323050,"updateDate":1730147323050}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323050, 1730147323050),
    ($SATQ$bce9910f$SATQ$, $SATQ$f0b28dae-ead4-424c-b282-82ad50054228$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p><em>Light of Truth</em> is a bronze and marble sculpture by artist Richard Hunt. It honors civil rights icon Ida B. Wells. The sculpture <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> in a tree-lined plaza in Chicago, just a few blocks from where Wells lived.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"c2a27c4b-5524-4c16-83a8-656fb6049d80","content":"<p>are standing</p>"},{"id":"ccc771d3-f857-43e1-b8a6-6df51411d3a2","content":"<p>have been standing</p>"},{"id":"ad955ccd-e063-43e2-8361-db9d370912b2","content":"<p>stands</p>"},{"id":"d179e32b-a43f-4657-b1af-66dd9bd0eefc","content":"<p>were standing</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["ad955ccd-e063-43e2-8361-db9d370912b2"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-verb agreement. The singular verb "stands" agrees in number with the singular subject "sculpture."</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the plural verb "are standing" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject "sculpture." Choice B is incorrect because the plural verb "have been standing" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject "sculpture." Choice D is incorrect because the plural verb "were standing" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject "sculpture."</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"bce9910f","external_id":"f0b28dae-ead4-424c-b282-82ad50054228","vaultid":null,"uId":"aa948479-8e22-4f8b-a1d4-6098cbe65c38","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p><em>Light of Truth</em> is a bronze and marble sculpture by artist Richard Hunt. It honors civil rights icon Ida B. Wells. The sculpture <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> in a tree-lined plaza in Chicago, just a few blocks from where Wells lived.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"c2a27c4b-5524-4c16-83a8-656fb6049d80","content":"<p>are standing</p>"},{"id":"ccc771d3-f857-43e1-b8a6-6df51411d3a2","content":"<p>have been standing</p>"},{"id":"ad955ccd-e063-43e2-8361-db9d370912b2","content":"<p>stands</p>"},{"id":"d179e32b-a43f-4657-b1af-66dd9bd0eefc","content":"<p>were standing</p>"}],"keys":["ad955ccd-e063-43e2-8361-db9d370912b2"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-verb agreement. The singular verb \"stands\" agrees in number with the singular subject \"sculpture.\"</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the plural verb \"are standing\" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject \"sculpture.\" Choice B is incorrect because the plural verb \"have been standing\" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject \"sculpture.\" Choice D is incorrect because the plural verb \"were standing\" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject \"sculpture.\"</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323051,"updateDate":1730147323051}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323051, 1730147323051),
    ($SATQ$bd11fe93$SATQ$, $SATQ$10ef5b9a-13eb-4311-bb29-32c9736b5614$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$5f5b4634-69d0-4d8e-a363-229fd36f6aa6$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Dr. Roc&iacute;o Paola Caballero-Gill is a paleoceanographer. This means that Dr. Caballero-Gill doesn&rsquo;t just study oceans as they are today. She uses chemistry and fossil evidence <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> oceans as they were in the past.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"e3f33840-07e6-4aa7-855c-65c2f18ab6cd","content":"<p>has studied</p>"},{"id":"fefecd91-fd50-4c54-a057-9450591e6220","content":"<p>to study</p>"},{"id":"220d1379-7bc3-45d7-a881-ce9f199b0077","content":"<p>studied</p>"},{"id":"da7af1e1-5e9c-4956-a367-843f750dbb70","content":"<p>studies</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["fefecd91-fd50-4c54-a057-9450591e6220"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of verb forms within a sentence. The nonfinite to-infinitive verb &ldquo;to study&rdquo; is correctly used to form a subordinate clause that indicates what Dr. Caballero-Gill uses chemistry and fossil evidence for (to study oceans as they were in the past). </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in an ungrammatical sentence. The finite verb &ldquo;has studied&rdquo; can&rsquo;t be used in this way to form a subordinate clause that indicates what Dr. Caballero-Gill uses the evidence for. Choice C is incorrect because it results in an ungrammatical sentence. The finite verb &ldquo;studied&rdquo; can&rsquo;t be used in this way to form a subordinate clause that indicates what Dr. Caballero-Gill uses the evidence for. Choice D is incorrect because it results in an ungrammatical sentence. The finite verb &ldquo;studies&rdquo; can&rsquo;t be used in this way to form a subordinate clause that indicates what Dr. Caballero-Gill uses the evidence for. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-737-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$a032c93b-b8c0-4f7e-b24b-bc9767d4bce8$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"bd11fe93","external_id":"10ef5b9a-13eb-4311-bb29-32c9736b5614","vaultid":"5f5b4634-69d0-4d8e-a363-229fd36f6aa6","uId":"fb96fa1e-a430-4e47-98ed-974787de6043","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":1,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Dr. Roc&iacute;o Paola Caballero-Gill is a paleoceanographer. This means that Dr. Caballero-Gill doesn&rsquo;t just study oceans as they are today. She uses chemistry and fossil evidence <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> oceans as they were in the past.&nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"e3f33840-07e6-4aa7-855c-65c2f18ab6cd","content":"<p>has studied</p>"},{"id":"fefecd91-fd50-4c54-a057-9450591e6220","content":"<p>to study</p>"},{"id":"220d1379-7bc3-45d7-a881-ce9f199b0077","content":"<p>studied</p>"},{"id":"da7af1e1-5e9c-4956-a367-843f750dbb70","content":"<p>studies</p>"}],"keys":["fefecd91-fd50-4c54-a057-9450591e6220"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of verb forms within a sentence. The nonfinite to-infinitive verb &ldquo;to study&rdquo; is correctly used to form a subordinate clause that indicates what Dr. Caballero-Gill uses chemistry and fossil evidence for (to study oceans as they were in the past). </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in an ungrammatical sentence. The finite verb &ldquo;has studied&rdquo; can&rsquo;t be used in this way to form a subordinate clause that indicates what Dr. Caballero-Gill uses the evidence for. Choice C is incorrect because it results in an ungrammatical sentence. The finite verb &ldquo;studied&rdquo; can&rsquo;t be used in this way to form a subordinate clause that indicates what Dr. Caballero-Gill uses the evidence for. Choice D is incorrect because it results in an ungrammatical sentence. The finite verb &ldquo;studies&rdquo; can&rsquo;t be used in this way to form a subordinate clause that indicates what Dr. Caballero-Gill uses the evidence for. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-737-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"a032c93b-b8c0-4f7e-b24b-bc9767d4bce8","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959349,"updateDate":1691007959349}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959349, 1691007959349),
    ($SATQ$be34a3df$SATQ$, $SATQ$e5b71b6d-2901-4d56-b764-950afb57a96b$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$708d7e9e-cd2c-42ce-9c7b-d6c99e0e134f$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In 2008, two years after the death of science fiction writer Octavia Butler, the Huntington Library in <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> received a collection of more than 8,000 items, including Butler&rsquo;s private notes, research materials, manuscripts, photos, and drawings. Today, the Octavia E. Butler Collection is one of the most researched archives at the library.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"3d6e4ecf-ea7f-4d19-8ffa-42681247c93e","content":"<p>California,</p>"},{"id":"96dc2ef7-6421-46fe-847c-a58a45a6cf2b","content":"<p>California:</p>"},{"id":"baabda6c-a471-4035-a19c-d4dc26b29917","content":"<p>California&mdash;</p>"},{"id":"ac291ccd-ef1a-4775-9b32-74b4cfa3fce6","content":"<p>California</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["ac291ccd-ef1a-4775-9b32-74b4cfa3fce6"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. No punctuation should separate the subject of the sentence (&ldquo;the Huntington Library in California&rdquo;) from its verb (&ldquo;received&rdquo;). </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. No punctuation should separate the subject of the sentence (&ldquo;the Huntington Library in California&rdquo;) from its verb (&ldquo;received&rdquo;). Choice B is incorrect. No punctuation should separate the subject of the sentence (&ldquo;the Huntington Library in California&rdquo;) from its verb (&ldquo;received&rdquo;). Choice C is incorrect. No punctuation should separate the subject of the sentence (&ldquo;the Huntington Library in California&rdquo;) from its verb (&ldquo;received&rdquo;). </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-BOU-M006$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$7d37af79-b009-4af8-92ab-c0a083046b67$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"be34a3df","external_id":"e5b71b6d-2901-4d56-b764-950afb57a96b","vaultid":"708d7e9e-cd2c-42ce-9c7b-d6c99e0e134f","uId":"4d6d6938-616c-4fb2-9206-6f22db555537","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In 2008, two years after the death of science fiction writer Octavia Butler, the Huntington Library in <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> received a collection of more than 8,000 items, including Butler&rsquo;s private notes, research materials, manuscripts, photos, and drawings. Today, the Octavia E. Butler Collection is one of the most researched archives at the library.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"3d6e4ecf-ea7f-4d19-8ffa-42681247c93e","content":"<p>California,</p>"},{"id":"96dc2ef7-6421-46fe-847c-a58a45a6cf2b","content":"<p>California:</p>"},{"id":"baabda6c-a471-4035-a19c-d4dc26b29917","content":"<p>California&mdash;</p>"},{"id":"ac291ccd-ef1a-4775-9b32-74b4cfa3fce6","content":"<p>California</p>"}],"keys":["ac291ccd-ef1a-4775-9b32-74b4cfa3fce6"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. No punctuation should separate the subject of the sentence (&ldquo;the Huntington Library in California&rdquo;) from its verb (&ldquo;received&rdquo;). </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. No punctuation should separate the subject of the sentence (&ldquo;the Huntington Library in California&rdquo;) from its verb (&ldquo;received&rdquo;). Choice B is incorrect. No punctuation should separate the subject of the sentence (&ldquo;the Huntington Library in California&rdquo;) from its verb (&ldquo;received&rdquo;). Choice C is incorrect. No punctuation should separate the subject of the sentence (&ldquo;the Huntington Library in California&rdquo;) from its verb (&ldquo;received&rdquo;). </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-BOU-M006","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"7d37af79-b009-4af8-92ab-c0a083046b67","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959359,"updateDate":1691007959359}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959359, 1691007959359),
    ($SATQ$be37d4ae$SATQ$, $SATQ$f7255304-c01b-4724-93c7-be429e27ea76$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>During the English neoclassical period (1660&ndash;1789), many writers imitated the epic poetry and satires of ancient Greece and Rome. They were not the first in England to adopt the literary modes of classical <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> some of the most prominent figures of the earlier Renaissance period were also influenced by ancient Greek and Roman literature.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"cd0f37b4-a216-48be-ad06-4240f9a43d12","content":"<p>antiquity<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span> however</p>"},{"id":"8c23a99e-a31c-4634-a659-85e6f59d302f","content":"<p>antiquity<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span> however<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span></p>"},{"id":"97c49433-7f57-4040-a796-184301a14b29","content":"<p>antiquity<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span> however<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">;</span></p>"},{"id":"e81ec5af-39ca-43f3-b31d-a72f17a53f48","content":"<p>antiquity<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">;</span> however<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["97c49433-7f57-4040-a796-184301a14b29"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is the punctuation of a supplementary phrase following a clause. This choice uses a comma to separate the supplementary adverb phrase "however" from the independent clause it modifies ("They...antiquity") and uses a semicolon to join the first independent clause ("They...antiquity") and the second independent clause ("some...literature"). Further, placing the semicolon after "however" indicates that the information in the clause that this is part of (that neoclassical writers were not the first to adopt classical literary modes) is contrary to what might be assumed from the information in the previous sentence (that the neoclassical writers were unique in imitating classical epic poetry and satires).</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it fails to mark the boundary after "however" between the two independent clauses with appropriate punctuation. Choice B is incorrect because the comma after "however" can&rsquo;t be used in this way to mark the boundary between the two independent clauses. Choice D is incorrect because placing the semicolon after "antiquity" illogically indicates that the information in the clause that this is part of (that prominent Renaissance figures were also influenced by classical literature) is contrary to the information in the previous clause (that neoclassical writers were not the first to adopt classical literary modes).</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"be37d4ae","external_id":"f7255304-c01b-4724-93c7-be429e27ea76","vaultid":null,"uId":"c10f4448-e12c-449d-9f81-d8d417a98140","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>During the English neoclassical period (1660&ndash;1789), many writers imitated the epic poetry and satires of ancient Greece and Rome. They were not the first in England to adopt the literary modes of classical <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> some of the most prominent figures of the earlier Renaissance period were also influenced by ancient Greek and Roman literature.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"cd0f37b4-a216-48be-ad06-4240f9a43d12","content":"<p>antiquity<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span> however</p>"},{"id":"8c23a99e-a31c-4634-a659-85e6f59d302f","content":"<p>antiquity<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span> however<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span></p>"},{"id":"97c49433-7f57-4040-a796-184301a14b29","content":"<p>antiquity<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span> however<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">;</span></p>"},{"id":"e81ec5af-39ca-43f3-b31d-a72f17a53f48","content":"<p>antiquity<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">;</span> however<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span></p>"}],"keys":["97c49433-7f57-4040-a796-184301a14b29"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is the punctuation of a supplementary phrase following a clause. This choice uses a comma to separate the supplementary adverb phrase \"however\" from the independent clause it modifies (\"They...antiquity\") and uses a semicolon to join the first independent clause (\"They...antiquity\") and the second independent clause (\"some...literature\"). Further, placing the semicolon after \"however\" indicates that the information in the clause that this is part of (that neoclassical writers were not the first to adopt classical literary modes) is contrary to what might be assumed from the information in the previous sentence (that the neoclassical writers were unique in imitating classical epic poetry and satires).</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it fails to mark the boundary after \"however\" between the two independent clauses with appropriate punctuation. Choice B is incorrect because the comma after \"however\" can&rsquo;t be used in this way to mark the boundary between the two independent clauses. Choice D is incorrect because placing the semicolon after \"antiquity\" illogically indicates that the information in the clause that this is part of (that prominent Renaissance figures were also influenced by classical literature) is contrary to the information in the previous clause (that neoclassical writers were not the first to adopt classical literary modes).</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1691007959345,"updateDate":1691007959345}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959345, 1691007959345),
    ($SATQ$c04e9136$SATQ$, $SATQ$cea96fdd-ebbd-442a-8b87-1b4e0568d15c$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$e1f8d681-2422-44e0-b384-915d9a729d7a$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The 2022 Nobel Prize in Chemistry was awarded to three pioneers in the field of click chemistry: two-time Nobel laureate Barry Sharpless, who coined the term &ldquo;click chemistry&rdquo; in 1998; Carolyn Bertozzi, founder of the Bertozzi Group at <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> and Morten Meldal, a professor at the University of Copenhagen in Denmark.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"cbca0d43-3c10-49b8-bcfd-a816d20173c9","content":"<p>Stanford</p>"},{"id":"877b8a89-faea-4a13-84ff-704dd022dc26","content":"<p>Stanford,</p>"},{"id":"a5822f47-733f-4640-905a-f20d7c7a5965","content":"<p>Stanford:</p>"},{"id":"b0bef182-5392-4168-b54f-3fdaf58fe755","content":"<p>Stanford;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["b0bef182-5392-4168-b54f-3fdaf58fe755"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. This choice correctly uses a semicolon to punctuate a complex list (which is made up of items that have commas in them).</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This is a complex list, indicated by the semicolon between "1998" and "Carolyn Bertozzi." What comes after the blank is a separate list item, so punctuation is needed after "Stanford." Choice B is incorrect. This is a complex list, indicated by the semicolon between "1998" and "Carolyn Bertozzi." The items in this list have internal punctuation in the form of commas and must therefore be separated by semicolons to avoid confusion. Choice C is incorrect. This is a complex list, indicated by the semicolon between "1998" and "Carolyn Bertozzi." Colons aren&rsquo;t used to separate items in a list.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-BOU-M009$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$60e5b2ab-e289-416e-89b6-c72d8fb273d5$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"c04e9136","external_id":"cea96fdd-ebbd-442a-8b87-1b4e0568d15c","vaultid":"e1f8d681-2422-44e0-b384-915d9a729d7a","uId":"e9e9b196-c46e-4046-ad0b-6043efa83ddb","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The 2022 Nobel Prize in Chemistry was awarded to three pioneers in the field of click chemistry: two-time Nobel laureate Barry Sharpless, who coined the term &ldquo;click chemistry&rdquo; in 1998; Carolyn Bertozzi, founder of the Bertozzi Group at <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> and Morten Meldal, a professor at the University of Copenhagen in Denmark.&nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"cbca0d43-3c10-49b8-bcfd-a816d20173c9","content":"<p>Stanford</p>"},{"id":"877b8a89-faea-4a13-84ff-704dd022dc26","content":"<p>Stanford,</p>"},{"id":"a5822f47-733f-4640-905a-f20d7c7a5965","content":"<p>Stanford:</p>"},{"id":"b0bef182-5392-4168-b54f-3fdaf58fe755","content":"<p>Stanford;</p>"}],"keys":["b0bef182-5392-4168-b54f-3fdaf58fe755"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. This choice correctly uses a semicolon to punctuate a complex list (which is made up of items that have commas in them).</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This is a complex list, indicated by the semicolon between \"1998\" and \"Carolyn Bertozzi.\" What comes after the blank is a separate list item, so punctuation is needed after \"Stanford.\" Choice B is incorrect. This is a complex list, indicated by the semicolon between \"1998\" and \"Carolyn Bertozzi.\" The items in this list have internal punctuation in the form of commas and must therefore be separated by semicolons to avoid confusion. Choice C is incorrect. This is a complex list, indicated by the semicolon between \"1998\" and \"Carolyn Bertozzi.\" Colons aren&rsquo;t used to separate items in a list.</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-BOU-M009","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"60e5b2ab-e289-416e-89b6-c72d8fb273d5","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959359,"updateDate":1691007959359}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959359, 1691007959359),
    ($SATQ$c06af4d8$SATQ$, $SATQ$a1fa2a51-c2ae-4892-8ffc-41238399100e$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$78ec09f7-dae9-45ba-bf85-1346c5f6c406$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Sociologist Alton Okinaka sits on the review board tasked with adding new sites to the Hawai&lsquo;i Register of Historic Places, which includes Pi&lsquo;ilanihale Heiau and the &lsquo;Ōpaeka&lsquo;a Road Bridge. Okinaka doesn&rsquo;t make such decisions <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> all historical designations must be approved by a group of nine other experts from the fields of architecture, archaeology, history, and Hawaiian culture.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"50c01ca2-37b1-473d-b2cb-b954a7eb889d","content":"<p>single-handedly, however;</p>"},{"id":"f8176a6b-352c-4139-9730-002652787692","content":"<p>single-handedly; however,</p>"},{"id":"8aa04568-b581-42a8-8a57-e87045f36886","content":"<p>single-handedly, however,</p>"},{"id":"948e599e-ec1b-4d00-849f-58b1ab79e8cd","content":"<p>single-handedly however</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["50c01ca2-37b1-473d-b2cb-b954a7eb889d"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is the punctuation of a supplementary word or phrase between two main clauses. This choice correctly uses a comma to separate the supplementary adverb &ldquo;however&rdquo; from the preceding main clause (&ldquo;Okinaka doesn&rsquo;t&hellip;single-handedly&rdquo;) and a semicolon to join the next main clause (&ldquo;all&hellip;culture&rdquo;) to the rest of the sentence. Further, placing the semicolon after &ldquo;however&rdquo; correctly indicates that the information in the preceding main clause (Okinaka doesn&rsquo;t make such decisions single-handedly) is contrary to what might be assumed from the information in the previous sentence (Okinaka sits on the review board that adds new sites to the Hawaii Register of Historic Places). </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because placing the semicolon after &ldquo;single-handedly&rdquo; and the comma after &ldquo;however&rdquo; illogically indicates that the information in the next main clause (all historical designations must be approved by a group of experts) is contrary to the information in the previous clause (Okinaka doesn&rsquo;t make such decisions single-handedly).&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. Commas can&rsquo;t be used in this way to punctuate a supplementary word or phrase between two main clauses.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect because it results in a run-on sentence. The two main clauses are fused without punctuation and/or a conjunction. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$T0014$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$13bec23c-0059-4e2f-8264-28884a8ee9d1$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$25$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"c06af4d8","external_id":"a1fa2a51-c2ae-4892-8ffc-41238399100e","vaultid":"78ec09f7-dae9-45ba-bf85-1346c5f6c406","uId":"54976eb3-2d1b-4fe0-a758-499aad64d62f","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Sociologist Alton Okinaka sits on the review board tasked with adding new sites to the Hawai&lsquo;i Register of Historic Places, which includes Pi&lsquo;ilanihale Heiau and the &lsquo;Ōpaeka&lsquo;a Road Bridge. Okinaka doesn&rsquo;t make such decisions <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> all historical designations must be approved by a group of nine other experts from the fields of architecture, archaeology, history, and Hawaiian culture.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"50c01ca2-37b1-473d-b2cb-b954a7eb889d","content":"<p>single-handedly, however;</p>"},{"id":"f8176a6b-352c-4139-9730-002652787692","content":"<p>single-handedly; however,</p>"},{"id":"8aa04568-b581-42a8-8a57-e87045f36886","content":"<p>single-handedly, however,</p>"},{"id":"948e599e-ec1b-4d00-849f-58b1ab79e8cd","content":"<p>single-handedly however</p>"}],"keys":["50c01ca2-37b1-473d-b2cb-b954a7eb889d"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is the punctuation of a supplementary word or phrase between two main clauses. This choice correctly uses a comma to separate the supplementary adverb &ldquo;however&rdquo; from the preceding main clause (&ldquo;Okinaka doesn&rsquo;t&hellip;single-handedly&rdquo;) and a semicolon to join the next main clause (&ldquo;all&hellip;culture&rdquo;) to the rest of the sentence. Further, placing the semicolon after &ldquo;however&rdquo; correctly indicates that the information in the preceding main clause (Okinaka doesn&rsquo;t make such decisions single-handedly) is contrary to what might be assumed from the information in the previous sentence (Okinaka sits on the review board that adds new sites to the Hawaii Register of Historic Places). </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because placing the semicolon after &ldquo;single-handedly&rdquo; and the comma after &ldquo;however&rdquo; illogically indicates that the information in the next main clause (all historical designations must be approved by a group of experts) is contrary to the information in the previous clause (Okinaka doesn&rsquo;t make such decisions single-handedly).&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. Commas can&rsquo;t be used in this way to punctuate a supplementary word or phrase between two main clauses.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect because it results in a run-on sentence. The two main clauses are fused without punctuation and/or a conjunction. </p>","parenttemplatename":"T0014","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"13bec23c-0059-4e2f-8264-28884a8ee9d1","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"25","createDate":1691007959378,"updateDate":1691007959378}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959378, 1691007959378),
    ($SATQ$c101fc44$SATQ$, $SATQ$7d35af27-68b0-465a-80d0-29820bbdaa6c$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$440412be-6d51-44b6-8890-a2c29f9af9ea$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>How do scientists determine what foods were eaten by extinct hominins such as Neanderthals? In the past, researchers were limited to studying the marks found on the fossilized teeth of skeletons, but in 2017 a team led by Laura Weyrich of the Australian Centre for Ancient DNA tried something <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> the DNA found in Neanderthals&rsquo; fossilized dental plaque.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"cba77574-e69e-40ae-83e3-69f54c03a3d2","content":"<p>new: sequencing</p>"},{"id":"c91ca2f0-3b75-4f9b-876a-038dbeb925f8","content":"<p>new; sequencing</p>"},{"id":"df48db44-b629-4e36-bef0-7ae9be0a8b14","content":"<p>new, sequencing:</p>"},{"id":"35e4c56e-f0b1-4982-92da-00e0ffc8fd8a","content":"<p>new. Sequencing</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["cba77574-e69e-40ae-83e3-69f54c03a3d2"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. &ldquo;A team&hellip;tried something new&rdquo; is an independent clause leading to an explanation of what the new thing was. A colon can only be used at the end of an independent clause, and typically introduces further explanation that expands upon the first clause, which makes a colon the perfect choice here. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. This choice results in a punctuation error. &ldquo;Sequencing&hellip;dental plaque&rdquo; can&rsquo;t stand on its own as a sentence, and so it can&rsquo;t be linked to the independent clause &ldquo;a team&hellip;tried something new&rdquo; with a semicolon. Only two independent clauses can be connected in this way. Choice C is incorrect. This choice results in a punctuation error. If &ldquo;sequencing&rdquo; is included in the first clause, it can no longer stand on its own as a complete idea. Since a colon can only come at the end of an independent clause, using one in this way creates an error. Choice D is incorrect. This choice results in a sentence fragment. &ldquo;Sequencing&hellip;dental plaque&rdquo; can&rsquo;t stand on its own as a sentence. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-BOU-M010$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$10c4ac11-51dc-465b-a48f-47a803e48804$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"c101fc44","external_id":"7d35af27-68b0-465a-80d0-29820bbdaa6c","vaultid":"440412be-6d51-44b6-8890-a2c29f9af9ea","uId":"bdbffc8a-070d-41a8-bd73-021bb7dea09a","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>How do scientists determine what foods were eaten by extinct hominins such as Neanderthals? In the past, researchers were limited to studying the marks found on the fossilized teeth of skeletons, but in 2017 a team led by Laura Weyrich of the Australian Centre for Ancient DNA tried something <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> the DNA found in Neanderthals&rsquo; fossilized dental plaque.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"cba77574-e69e-40ae-83e3-69f54c03a3d2","content":"<p>new: sequencing</p>"},{"id":"c91ca2f0-3b75-4f9b-876a-038dbeb925f8","content":"<p>new; sequencing</p>"},{"id":"df48db44-b629-4e36-bef0-7ae9be0a8b14","content":"<p>new, sequencing:</p>"},{"id":"35e4c56e-f0b1-4982-92da-00e0ffc8fd8a","content":"<p>new. Sequencing</p>"}],"keys":["cba77574-e69e-40ae-83e3-69f54c03a3d2"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. &ldquo;A team&hellip;tried something new&rdquo; is an independent clause leading to an explanation of what the new thing was. A colon can only be used at the end of an independent clause, and typically introduces further explanation that expands upon the first clause, which makes a colon the perfect choice here. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. This choice results in a punctuation error. &ldquo;Sequencing&hellip;dental plaque&rdquo; can&rsquo;t stand on its own as a sentence, and so it can&rsquo;t be linked to the independent clause &ldquo;a team&hellip;tried something new&rdquo; with a semicolon. Only two independent clauses can be connected in this way. Choice C is incorrect. This choice results in a punctuation error. If &ldquo;sequencing&rdquo; is included in the first clause, it can no longer stand on its own as a complete idea. Since a colon can only come at the end of an independent clause, using one in this way creates an error. Choice D is incorrect. This choice results in a sentence fragment. &ldquo;Sequencing&hellip;dental plaque&rdquo; can&rsquo;t stand on its own as a sentence. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-BOU-M010","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"10c4ac11-51dc-465b-a48f-47a803e48804","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959359,"updateDate":1691007959359}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959359, 1691007959359),
    ($SATQ$c102f9b0$SATQ$, $SATQ$2c5ae910-ddc7-449f-b570-d39c820b417b$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>L&ecirc; Lương Minh became the thirteenth secretary-general of the Association of Southeast Asian Nations (ASEAN) in January 2013, making <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> the first time the organization would appoint a Vietnamese leader.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"1eb0afb6-5f22-4311-80eb-83f2eb53a33f","content":"<p>these</p>"},{"id":"f5a6a8c6-1a10-4cf1-9b57-c9937656fd86","content":"<p>those</p>"},{"id":"23c0b2d7-b524-43ab-895d-def516e5ce97","content":"<p>this</p>"},{"id":"b3853456-b3dc-43a1-8cc7-188b7dce6c8b","content":"<p>some</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["23c0b2d7-b524-43ab-895d-def516e5ce97"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is pronoun-antecedent agreement. The singular pronoun "this" agrees in number with the singular antecedent "L&ecirc; Lương Minh became the thirteenth secretary-general of the Association of Southeast Asian Nations (ASEAN) in January 2013." The pronoun "this" is referring back to the singular event described earlier in the sentence in which Minh became secretary-general of ASEAN.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the plural pronoun "these" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular antecedent "L&ecirc; Lương Minh became the thirteenth secretary-general of the Association of Southeast Asian Nations (ASEAN) in January 2013." Choice B is incorrect because the plural pronoun "those" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular antecedent "L&ecirc; Lương Minh became the thirteenth secretary-general of the Association of Southeast Asian Nations (ASEAN) in January 2013." Choice D is incorrect because the indefinite pronoun "some" is ambiguous in this context; the resulting sentence leaves unclear what marks the first time the organization appointed a Vietnamese leader.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"c102f9b0","external_id":"2c5ae910-ddc7-449f-b570-d39c820b417b","vaultid":null,"uId":"8469b695-62fa-4cd4-99d0-1f1e1133e88e","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>L&ecirc; Lương Minh became the thirteenth secretary-general of the Association of Southeast Asian Nations (ASEAN) in January 2013, making <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> the first time the organization would appoint a Vietnamese leader.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"1eb0afb6-5f22-4311-80eb-83f2eb53a33f","content":"<p>these</p>"},{"id":"f5a6a8c6-1a10-4cf1-9b57-c9937656fd86","content":"<p>those</p>"},{"id":"23c0b2d7-b524-43ab-895d-def516e5ce97","content":"<p>this</p>"},{"id":"b3853456-b3dc-43a1-8cc7-188b7dce6c8b","content":"<p>some</p>"}],"keys":["23c0b2d7-b524-43ab-895d-def516e5ce97"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is pronoun-antecedent agreement. The singular pronoun \"this\" agrees in number with the singular antecedent \"L&ecirc; Lương Minh became the thirteenth secretary-general of the Association of Southeast Asian Nations (ASEAN) in January 2013.\" The pronoun \"this\" is referring back to the singular event described earlier in the sentence in which Minh became secretary-general of ASEAN.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the plural pronoun \"these\" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular antecedent \"L&ecirc; Lương Minh became the thirteenth secretary-general of the Association of Southeast Asian Nations (ASEAN) in January 2013.\" Choice B is incorrect because the plural pronoun \"those\" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular antecedent \"L&ecirc; Lương Minh became the thirteenth secretary-general of the Association of Southeast Asian Nations (ASEAN) in January 2013.\" Choice D is incorrect because the indefinite pronoun \"some\" is ambiguous in this context; the resulting sentence leaves unclear what marks the first time the organization appointed a Vietnamese leader.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323048,"updateDate":1730147323048}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323048, 1730147323048)
